.LSOverride
Icon?
.fseventsd
.TemporaryItems

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Build outputs (everything lands under build/)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
/build/
/out/
/dist/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Object / library artifacts (in case anything escapes build/)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.lib
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb
*.dSYM/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiler caches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
/.ccache/
.ccache/
ccache/
sccache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Tooling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
compile_commands.json
.cache/
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
tags
TAGS


# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Package-manager noise. This project has no npm dependencies;
# package.json is only a script runner, so a lockfile would carry
# no information and churn on every run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json
yarn.lock

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Benchmark build output: third-party libraries built in place, and
# the scenario binaries. Results are tracked; build products are not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
/bench/build/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Third-party source trees. Kept on disk to read and to build
# against; never part of our history.
#
# Deliberately file-by-file rather than one /bench/ rule: the notes
# and glue that sit next to a vendored tree are ours, and rules that
# are wider than the thing they describe are how a project ends up
# ignoring its own work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
/refs/
/bench/bdwgc/gc-*/
.eta/
